## AI Agents (Local Backend)

### ImageContextRetrieverAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/image_context_retriever_agent.py` (called from `context_builder.py`)

Takes a screenshot/image (base64) and extracts structured context:
- Screens detected
- UI elements visible
- User flows implied
- Requirements inferred

Returns prose context for each image.

### _SynthAgent
**File:** `backend/main.py` (inline usage)

Synthesises multiple image contexts + text notes into a single cohesive `context_summary` prose paragraph. Also used for feedback-based context refinement (skips image re-analysis when `user_feedback` is provided).

### TestPlannerAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/test_planner_agent.py`

Generates test cases from feature context. Accepts:
- `context_summary` (feature)
- `project_context` (project-level background)
- `user_feedback` (optional correction to steer generation)

Returns JSON: `{ feature_summary, test_cases[], coverage_notes }`.

Each test case: `{ test_key, title, goal, description, category, priority, expected_result }`.

**Default model:** `claude-haiku-4-5` (cost-optimised for text generation).

### ProjectContextLearnerAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/project_context_learner_agent.py`

Runs once after a non-aborted cloud-backed suite. Despite the historical class name, it
maintains both scopes in one classification pass:

- Rewrites the complete `Feature.context_summary`, preserving its existing source-derived
  description and adding concise bullets for newly observed feature behavior.
- Rewrites `Project.context_summary` only when the run established a durable capability or
  fact useful outside this feature; at most two short global additions are allowed.
- Treats explicit operator corrections as authoritative, but excludes supplied personal or
  secret values, transient UI/session state, test outcomes, and run narration.
- Deduplicates both summaries instead of appending raw run logs indefinitely.

### DocumentContextRetrieverAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/document_context_retriever_agent.py`

Extracts structured context from uploaded PDF/document files. Called during feature context build when documents are part of the assets.

### VideoContextRetrieverAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/video_context_retriever_agent.py`

Extracts structured context from uploaded video files (frame sampling + analysis). Called during feature context build when videos are part of the assets.

### ClaudeComputerUseAgent
**File:** `backend/agents/claude_computer_use_agent.py`

Production execution agent. Uses Anthropic's native Computer Use beta to control any macOS application via pixel-accurate coordinates. Unlike Gemini, it works on any desktop app, not just browsers.

**Key behaviours:**
- Accepts an optional `system_prompt` at init (the rich `cu_system_prompt` built from project + feature context)
- `start(goal, screenshot_bytes)` — begins a task, returns `ClaudeCUResponse`
- `step(tool_use_ids, screenshot_bytes, tool_errors=None)` — continues after executing actions; accepts a list of `tool_use_id`s (one per action in the previous response), sends them all back with the same final screenshot, and returns blocked or failed actions as error tool results
- `inject_guidance(text)` — queues operator guidance to be sent in the next `step()` call as a top-level user message text block
- Exposes `set_text_field({ coordinate, field_label, value })`,
  `request_user_input({ message })`, and `complete_test_execution(...)` alongside
  `computer` during the interactive loop; parsed requests/results are returned on
  `ClaudeCUResponse`
- `answer_user_input(tool_use_id, value)` — appends the operator's value as the matching custom-tool result in the same `self.messages` conversation, then calls Claude again
- Runs synchronous Anthropic API calls via `await asyncio.to_thread()` to avoid blocking the FastAPI event loop (critical for pause/abort to work)
- Maintains the complete per-test Anthropic message history in `self.messages`; every `_call()` resends that history, including previous screenshot tool results
- When `CLAUDE_CU_DEBUG_MESSAGES=true`, logs the sanitized request structure before each API call and response token usage afterward. Base64 image data and values supplied through `request_user_input` are redacted, but other text goals and operator guidance remain visible and may be sensitive.
- Enables Anthropic's automatic five-minute prompt cache on every Computer Use request via `cache_control={"type": "ephemeral"}`. Live verification showed `cache_read_input_tokens` increasing on later turns, confirming that the growing conversation prefix is served as discounted cache reads.

**Default model:** `claude-haiku-4-5`
**Beta flag:** `computer-use-2025-01-24`
**Tool version:** `computer_20250124`

**Multi-action batching (`BATCHING_INSTRUCTIONS` constant):**

A module-level constant `BATCHING_INSTRUCTIONS` is defined in this file and appended to both `DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT` (fallback) and the runtime-built `cu_system_prompt` in `main.py`. It instructs Claude to:
- Pack multiple confident actions into one response (for example, fill several already
  visible fields with separate `set_text_field` calls)
- Return only 1 action when the next step depends on visual feedback (navigation, form submit, modal trigger)
- Use the native `screenshot` action for an explicit mid-sequence visual checkpoint

The execution loop in `main.py` handles N actions per turn: it iterates
`cu_response.actions`, executes each, collects all `tool_use_id`s, then calls
`agent.step(all_ids, one_screenshot)` once. If an action fails, its own tool result is
returned to Claude as text with `is_error: true`; later actions in that batch are not
executed and receive dependent-error results instead of false success screenshots.

Each action in a batch still gets its own individual `StepRecord` in the DB (own `step_number`, `timestamp`, `success`, `error`). All actions in the same turn share the same `reasoning`/`description` text since that comes from `cu_response.thinking` (per-turn, not per-action).

**Operator input (`USER_INPUT_INSTRUCTIONS` + `REQUEST_USER_INPUT_TOOL`):**

Both the fallback `DEFAULT_SYSTEM_PROMPT` and the runtime-built prompt in `main.py`
include the shared `USER_INPUT_INSTRUCTIONS` constant. `_configure_screenshot_space()`
places `REQUEST_USER_INPUT_TOOL` next to the native Computer Use tool. This makes the
tool available from the first screenshot through every later turn of a test.

**Atomic text-field editing (`FORM_EDITING_INSTRUCTIONS` + `SET_TEXT_FIELD_TOOL`):**

Both prompts tell Claude that it is controlling macOS and to use `Command`, not
`Control`, for standard editing shortcuts. Generic `computer` key actions are validated
against the keys supported by PyAutoGUI and may contain only one key or one chord;
malformed strings that combine sequential operations are returned to Claude as action
errors rather than being treated as successful input.

For the common clear-or-replace operation, Claude uses one custom call:

```json
{
  "name": "set_text_field",
  "input": {
    "coordinate": [640, 412],
    "field_label": "Email",
    "value": "person@example.com"
  }
}
```

`coordinate` is the field centre in Claude's current screenshot. The agent passes it
through the same screenshot-to-window coordinate conversion used by native Computer Use
clicks; the executor then adds the selected window's screen offset. The backend performs
one click, `Command+A`, Backspace, and (unless `value` is empty) types the full value.
An empty string therefore clears the field. `field_label` identifies the intended field
in logs and provenance checks; the coordinate remains the actual click target.

This keeps the multi-step editing mechanics inside Clariti while still producing one
tool result for Claude. The existing sensitive-input guard still decides whether the
provided value came from project/test memory, the goal, explicit fake-data instructions,
or an operator answer. Focus leases and screenshot-based no-op detection are not part of
this implementation.

